From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:45:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 040/184] segacd: lower the bus-interlock flag again

An edit a few builds ago removed the line that clears it, so it was
raised on the first exchange and stayed raised. Nothing complained,
because a flag that is never lowered is indistinguishable from a
machine that is always busy: the servant's polite grab found it set
every time, fell back to its four-frame override, and took the bus
without regard for the drive from then on. The screen also ran at a
fifth of its rate, silently.

The status line had been reporting it since it was introduced -- the
probe digit reads 8 when this bit is stuck, and every hardware report
since has read 8.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 7249963..aa62611 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -427,6 +427,15 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
     if (cdd_sent < 0xFF)
         cdd_sent++;
     cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(*CDD_CTRL_RD & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
+
+    /* Done: the Genesis may have the bus again. An edit lost this line
+     * a few builds ago and nothing complained, because a flag that is
+     * raised and never lowered looks exactly like a machine that is
+     * always busy: the interlock stopped protecting anything, the
+     * servant fell back to grabbing anyway every fifth frame, and the
+     * screen quietly ran at a fifth of its rate. The status line said
+     * so all along -- the probe digit reads 8 when this bit is stuck. */
+    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 &= (UWORD)~0x0008;
     /* A boolean cannot tell a dead link from a flaky one. Count how
      * many of the last sixteen packets checksummed and report that:
      * F is a clean link, 0 is nothing, anything between is a race. */
